Getting blasted:

    When you pick up or wield an artifact that has an alignment or a
character class different from your own, you will be blasted by its
power.  If the artifact is self-willed (i.e. a quest object), and both
the alignment and the class are different from yours, the object will
also evade your grasp.  Otherwise, if you don't mind being blasted, you
can use the artifact.
** For these purposes, Excalibur does not discriminate between character
classes.

Invocation:

    Most quest objects do something special when invoked.  Some toggle
a property, such as invisibility.  Others perform a task.  Both kinds
of invocation are limited; you have to wait a certain number of moves
between invocations.  However, if you've turned a property on, you
don't have to wait to turn it off.  Once it's turned off, you do have
to wait before you can turn it on again.  The number of moves is on
the order of a hundred, and increases with your experience level.

Explanation of special effects:

  dungeon teleport - allows you to teleport to (the closest level in)
        any dungeon you've already visited.  You get to pick from a menu.

  healing - restores half of your hp deficit.

  restore magical energy - restores half of your energy deficit.

  half damg, half spdm - reduces damage (physical or magical, respectively)
        to your person by half.

  speak - apparently not implemented yet.

  Magicbane - can probe, stun, scare, or purge (= cancel) a monster.
        The chance of these special attacks occuring goes down
        as the weapon is enchanted.


